  :adr/body "# Full public-reference genetics into rasen, with kotobase.net as the query face

## The question that started it

\"Is all the human DNA data in kotobase.net?\" No, and three separate layers were
in the way. Measuring each of them, rather than answering from the shape of the
repo, is what produced this decision.

**Layer 1 — rasen does not write to kotobase.net at all.** `grep -rn kotobase`
over `src/`, `tools/` and the EDN roots of `etzhayyim/actor-rasen` returns zero
hits. The file named `kotoba.cljc` is a local append-only ledger writer that
states \"performs NO network I/O\"; publication goes to IPFS pin + IPNS. The
kotobase.net tenant plane was never wired as a destination.

**Layer 2 — the corpus itself was 53 nodes.** Measured in
`data/seed-genome-graph.kotoba.edn`: 16 gene, 14 phenotype, 12 variant, 6
population, 5 pathway; the persisted ledger carries `:tx/count 583`.

**Layer 3 — the path that would grow it does not run.** `ingest.cljc` refuses
live ingest (\"REFUSED: live gene ingest (MyGene.info / MyVariant.info) is
G7-gated network + not ported\"); only `--offline` re-addressing works. The
Python original it was ported from is not in the repository. Upstream is fine:
mygene.info and myvariant.info both answer 200. What is missing is one unported
path, not egress and not the sources.

## Decision

Ingest the **full public reference corpus** — ClinVar in its entirety, gnomAD
population-aggregate allele frequencies — and keep **individual genotypes out**,
which is not a scope cut but the actor's constitution (G1: no individual
genotype registry, no identifiable personal sequence, no sample- or family-level
data; population frequencies at super-population scale only; location as COARSE
cytoband only).

Destination is **both faces, with an explicit direction**: the append-only
content-addressed ledger stays canonical, and kotobase.net carries the same
datoms as a **projection** — deletable and rebuildable from the ledger, per the
superproject's projection rule. kotobase.net is where Datalog reaches the graph;
it is never where the graph lives.

## What the scale actually is (measured, not estimated from reputation)

| source | measured |
|---|---|
| ClinVar `variant_summary.txt.gz` | 442,495,433 bytes (2026-08-31) |
| ClinVar GRCh38 VCF | 193,427,450 bytes |
| gnomAD v4.1 genomes, **chr21 alone** | 7,758,981,978 bytes |
| gnomAD v4.1 exomes, **chr21 alone** | 2,250,319,809 bytes |

chr21 is among the smallest chromosomes. The full gnomAD sites release is
hundreds of gigabytes. That settles a design question rather than describing
one: **the raw VCFs are not ingested.** Only per-variant super-population
allele frequencies are extracted, which is also all G1 permits. Any raw bulk
that must be retained goes to B2 + DataLad, never into git.

Normalising 5,282 real ClinVar lines through the new code: 2,619 GRCh38 rows
kept, 2,663 skipped, 4,365 nodes and 9,076 edges, 0 G1 violations. Kept plus
skipped equals rows read. Extrapolated to the full release that is roughly 5.8M
lines, about 2.9M GRCh38 rows, on the order of 10M edges and tens of millions of
datoms.

**Consequence the measurement forces:** the ledger is one EDN file holding 583
datoms today. It cannot hold that. Sharding of the ledger is a prerequisite for
the full run, not an optimisation of it.

## Landed in this step

`rasen.methods.clinvar` (etzhayyim/actor-rasen main a261f970) — the full-corpus
normaliser, emitting the same node and edge shapes as the bounded producer so
the two are interchangeable downstream.

G1 is enforced as a check rather than described in a comment. `variant_summary`
carries Chromosome / Start / Stop / PositionVCF; the ontology permits coarse
cytoband only. `g1-violations` refuses any node carrying a coordinate attr,
`row->graph` runs it over everything it emits, and it throws rather than
reporting clean when handed a non-map, so an unreadable node is UNVERIFIED and
not silently clean. The negative test pins the refusal reason, so an unrelated
failure cannot be counted as a successful discrimination.

The suite caught one real defect: MONDO ids were built with a doubled prefix
(`ph.mondo-mondo-0011450`), which would have split every MONDO condition into
two node ids — one from this producer, one from `ingest` — and the split would
have been invisible in both. MONDO values carry their own prefix.

Skipped rows return nil rather than an empty graph, and the accumulator reports
rows and skips, so coverage cannot be silently overstated.

## Sharding — landed (rasen main a979d183)

`rasen.methods.ledger-shards`. Sharding does not touch the chain: a tx cid is
sha256 over (datoms, prev-cid) and nothing else, so splitting the same linear
sequence across files preserves every cid. `migrate!` asserts that instead of
assuming it; on the real on-disk ledger (1 tx, 583 datoms) the head comes
through byte-identical as b17a2fa1…d99990.

The failure worth engineering against is not splitting but INCOMPLETENESS. Once
the log is many files, walking the shards you can find yields a shorter chain
that verifies perfectly — \"could not read shard 7\" returns the same ok as \"read
every shard and all linked\". The index is therefore the evidence floor:
`verify!` reports ok only after seeing exactly as many txs as the index says
exist, and it separates :no-index, :index-invalid, :shard-missing,
:chain-broken and :count-mismatch instead of collapsing them into false.
`index-violations` throws for a non-index rather than reporting no violations.
`reindex!` refuses to synthesise an index for an empty directory. `append-txs!`
refuses a run that does not continue the head.

Both refusal paths were broken deliberately and only their own tests failed:
skipping unreadable shards failed the missing-shard tests, and removing the
count floor failed the truncation test alone.

**A defect in the existing ledger, found by trying to migrate it.**
`kotoba/verify-chain` returned `{:ok true :length 0}` for a path that does not
exist. `read-log` returning [] for a missing file is correct — `autorun` starts
a fresh chain from genesis on a machine with no ledger — but folding that []
into verification made \"there is no ledger here\" indistinguishable from \"I
walked the whole ledger and every link holds\". It now refuses with :no-log.
This is the same class the superproject CLAUDE.md records, sitting in the
ledger's own verifier.

Two writing hazards worth carrying forward. `pr-str` cannot write the index: it
collapses same-namespace maps to `#:index{…}`, which the minimal reader in
`kotoba.cljc` parses to the string \"#:index\" — silently, yielding an index that
is not a map. And `data/persisted/` is gitignored by design (machine-local,
regenerable from the committed seed), so a worktree has no ledger at all; that
absence is exactly what surfaced the verifier defect.

The actual migration of the live ledger is deliberately NOT done here. It
belongs with the streaming runner — migrating with nothing to append is churn,
and `publish.cljc` / `datom-emit` still read the single file.

## Streaming runner — landed (rasen main ceeb9e87)

`rasen.methods.clinvar-run`. Acquisition is separate from normalisation: a full release takes
long enough that holding one HTTP connection across it is its own failure mode. The declared
size is passed in by the caller rather than read from a response header, because babashka —
which runs this repository — does not permit the HttpURLConnection header methods, and a size
check that silently does nothing on the runtime we actually use is worse than none. Resume is
keyed on the sha256 of the downloaded release, not Last-Modified: row offsets are only
meaningful against the exact bytes they were counted in.

:complete is returned only when the stream can be shown to have ended where the source said —
gzip's own trailer validating, and a declared size matching. Otherwise :truncated. A bounded
run returns :limited and can never claim the whole corpus.

Measured against the live release (442,495,433 bytes, sha256:186ad283…04c9): fetch verified in
21s; 40,414 rows read / 19,999 kept / 20,415 skipped in 180s = 224 rows/s; 560,445 datoms in
28 txs across 3 shards; chain verifying.

**The full run has not been done, and not for a reason in the code.** Extrapolated it is ~5.8M
rows, ~7.2 hours, ~81M datoms, ~6.2 GB of ledger. This machine has 2.3 GB free of 927 GB
(measured 2026-09-06, disk at 100%). Starting it would fill the disk and take every other
session on this host down with it. The run needs either space here or the ledger on B2/DataLad
per the superproject's large-binary rule — a storage decision, taken before the run, not
during it.

## kotobase projection — seam landed, transport blocked (rasen main 480f3656)

`rasen.methods.kotobase-publish` carries no transport and no credential; writes go through an
injected (fn [tx-edn] -> result). `kotoba-lang/kotobase-client` is the canonical client and is
what a host wires this to — rasen runs under bb and that client is ClojureScript, and the seam
exists so rasen does not grow a second one.

The watermark is the part worth engineering. Publish a batch, advance, repeat — and a
transport returning nil, or a 202 never applied, advances it exactly as far as a real
acknowledgement does; the next run starts after datoms that were never stored and no later run
goes back for them. So `acknowledged?` recognises success rather than assuming it, the run
stops at the first unrecognised reply, and the resumed run re-sends that transaction rather
than the one after it. The ledger is verified before anything is sent: projecting a ledger that
does not verify would put datoms on the target that no shard accounts for, ending the
rebuildability that makes it a projection at all.

**Both faces of kotobase.net are currently unreachable, measured 2026-09-06.** POST
/api/transact without credentials returns 401. The datom plane went Biscuit-required
(net-kotobase ADR-2608280230) and CACAO no longer authenticates ordinary tenant reads or
writes. The operator seed KOTOBA_SEED is a write-only Cloudflare Worker secret with no readable
copy anywhere — by design, and changing it would change the operator DID and therefore the
graph namespace. The archive plane's KOTOBASE_ARCHIVE_TOKEN returns `no such item` from the
kagi vault today, the third swing for that one item; the secrets map has been corrected. Minting
or guessing a credential is not something an agent does (safety floor 1), so this is owner
action: issue a Biscuit for a rasen tenant, or restore the archive token.

Entity identity is the target's property. Ledger ids are content-stable strings; whether a
target upserts on one or mints a fresh entity per transaction cannot be checked without a live
endpoint, so `publish!` does not claim idempotence and `:entity-fn` is where a host supplies
what its target needs.

## gnomAD frequencies — landed, and G1 caps the coverage (rasen main 480f3656)

`rasen.methods.gnomad` emits population nodes and :allele-frequency 縁 and nothing else.

Two constraints here are constitutional rather than technical, and both came from reading real
gnomAD rather than the spec.

A VCF record identifies a variant by CHROM/POS/REF/ALT. The only G1-safe identity it carries is
its dbSNP rsID, and gnomAD leaves that empty for a large share of records — measured on a real
v4.1 chr21 genomes prefix, 24,189 of 41,172 (58.8%). Those records are not representable at
all. So `extract` partitions every record into :with-rsid and :without-rsid and `coverage`
returns nil rather than a number when nothing was read: **full gnomAD coverage is false by
construction**, and the counts exist so it cannot be claimed by accident.

gnomAD publishes AF_XX and AF_XY — sex-stratified — beside the super-population keys, plus
AF_fin / AF_asj / AF_ami / AF_mid / AF_remaining, all finer than :population/code admits. The
mapping is an explicit allow-list, not a prefix match; a prefix match would sweep them in and
the resulting edges would be indistinguishable from legitimate ones.

Measured on the real prefix: 41,172 records → 21,573 distinct variants, 144,544 frequency edges
across exactly six populations, 0 G1 violations, 5.4s (~7,600 records/s).

**Scale, so nobody plans against the wrong number.** That 25 MB prefix is 0.32% of chr21
genomes alone (7.76 GB). gnomAD v4.1 genomes is on the order of 760M variants; at 58.8%
representable that is ~450M variants and billions of frequency edges — orders of magnitude
beyond ClinVar's ~81M datoms, and not a run this host can hold under any arrangement. It needs
the storage decision first, and probably a different machine.

## R2 — the storage blocker is gone, and the run is under way (rasen main 079e89ca)

`rasen.methods.shard-archive`. A sealed shard is immutable, so it lives in an object store and
comes back when something reads it. `archive!` reads every upload BACK and sha256-compares it
before removing the local file: a PUT returning 200 for a body that was never stored, a partial
write, a bucket that drops it — each would otherwise end with the canonical bytes gone and the
ledger discovering it at the next verify, with nothing to restore from. Only sealed shards are
touched; the last one is still open for appends, and uploading it would store a prefix and call
it the shard.

**Demonstrated end to end on real R2, not on a mock.** With shard 0 freed locally, `verify!`
refuses with :shard-missing rather than verifying the shorter chain; restoring it from R2
returns {:ok true :tx-count 18 :shards 2}. The object is 15,821,265 bytes and the manifest
carries its sha256.

The source streams from HTTP now, for a measured reason: landing the 442 MB release first
failed with ENOSPC mid-download. Free space on this host moved between 125 MB and 11 GB during
the work, driven by other processes. Completeness for a stream rests on gzip's trailer — CRC32
and uncompressed length, validated at end of stream — which is a stronger check than a byte
count, not a weaker one. Passing :expect-bytes with a URL is refused rather than ignored.

Steady-state local footprint is one open shard: ~10-30 MB, against ~6.2 GB if nothing were
offloaded. **The full ClinVar run is streaming as of 2026-09-06**, resumable from its
checkpoint, at ~252 rows/s.

## Cloudflare R2 — how the credential actually works

Three things the secrets map did not say, all measured 2026-09-06 and written back into it.

`--region auto` must be passed explicitly. This host's aws config sets ap-northeast-1, SigV4
puts the region in the signing scope, and R2 answers SignatureDoesNotMatch — **a correct key
looks revoked**. That misdiagnosis was made once here before the region was pinned.

The working path is a temp credential: keychain gftd.cf/API_TOKEN against
POST /accounts/<acct>/r2/temp-access-credentials, scoped to one bucket, object-read-write, up
to 7 days. Better than a long-lived account key, not worse. The kagi
`CLOUDFLARE_R2_ACCESS_KEY` is present again (the map said absent) but only half-valid: its
secret fails even with the region pinned, while its access-key-id is accepted as the temp
credential's parent.

`GET /user/tokens/verify` returns \"Invalid API Token\" for this account-scoped token while its
R2 calls succeed. A failed verify there is not evidence the token is dead.

## Not done, and blocking

1. **A credential for kotobase.net.** Owner action — the datom plane is Biscuit-required and
   the operator seed is a write-only Cloudflare secret by design; the archive token reads
   `no such item` today. This is the only thing still blocking the projection half.
2. **gnomAD at scale.** The extractor is verified on real records, but v4.1 genomes is on the
   order of 760M variants, ~58.8% of them representable, and that is a different order of
   magnitude from ClinVar. It needs its own run plan on top of the R2 offload.
3. **Migrating the live single-file ledger** onto shards, with `publish.cljc` / `datom-emit`
   pointed at them.

## Notes for whoever continues

The ADR number in the source docstrings of `clinvar.cljc` and
`test_clinvar.cljc` briefly said 2609061800, which belongs to a different ADR
(kotoba-web-architecture-fit). Corrected in rasen main 16d55c50.

While landing this, a superproject EDN document written by another loop
(`90-docs/business/revenue-agent-loop/runs/0037-kotobase-stab-001.edn`) was
found broken by four raw quotes inside a body string, which closed it early and
left the remainder to be read as symbols. Escaping restored it; content
unchanged. This is the failure mode the superproject CLAUDE.md already records,
and it recurred in a document written the same day.

## Closing state (2026-09-06)

**Landed.** `etzhayyim/actor-rasen` main `96d7d172`; the west pin and the shared checkout
both sit on it, so checkout / pin / main are one thing. Suite: **148 tests, 905 assertions,
0 failures**. Every refusal path added in this ADR's work was broken on purpose and only its
own test failed.

**The corpus is being built, and it is resumable.** Ledger at `~/.gftd/rasen-genome/ledger`
— durable, not a session scratchpad, because a resident bot cannot own state that disappears
with whoever started it. Sealed shards live in R2 `etzhayyim-rasen-genome` under
`rasen-genome/`. Measured at close: **723,378 rows read, 238,439 kept, 459 transactions,
9,187,054 datoms, 45 shards in R2, 14 MB on local disk** against the ~6.2 GB the corpus would
be if nothing were offloaded.

**The interruption handling was proven by the failure it was written for.** The live stream
was cut twice. The first time predated the fix and died as an unclassified
`SocketException`. The second time it produced
`run!: the source connection was interrupted — refusing to report a corpus`, wrote
`:run/status :interrupted`, and kept `rows/read 717069` — so the resumed run picked up where
it stopped instead of re-reading a third of the release.

### Resume point

```bash
# credentials: bucket-scoped, object-read-write, 7 days from 2026-09-06
#   security find-generic-password -s gftd.cf -a API_TOKEN -w
#   -> POST /accounts/<acct>/r2/temp-access-credentials
#   ALWAYS pass --region auto; without it SigV4 signs for the config's region and R2
#   answers SignatureDoesNotMatch, which makes a correct key look revoked.
cd <a worktree of etzhayyim/actor-rasen at main>
. /tmp/r2env.sh                      # or mint fresh credentials as above
LEDGER_ROOT=$HOME/.gftd/rasen-genome/ledger \\
CV_FP=clinvar-2026-08-31-sha186ad283 \\
  bb <r2run.clj>                     # streams the release, resumes from the checkpoint
```

The run script is not in the repository — it is 20 lines of host glue that wires
`clinvar-run/run!` to `aws s3api` through `shard-archive`'s injected `store-fn`. Rewriting it
is a five-minute job and it deliberately carries the credential handling that the repo does
not.

### What blocks each remaining piece

1. **ClinVar completion** — nothing but time and connection stability. The run is streaming
   and resumes on its own.
2. **kotobase.net projection** — owner action. The datom plane is Biscuit-required, the
   operator seed is a write-only Cloudflare secret by design, and the archive token reads
   `no such item`. An agent does not mint or guess a credential.
3. **gnomAD** — needs its own run plan. The extractor is verified on real records; the corpus
   is on the order of 760M variants with 58.8% representable, which is a different order of
   magnitude from ClinVar's ~81M datoms.
4. **The ledger migration** of the old single-file ledger, with `publish.cljc` / `datom-emit`
   repointed at the shards.

### Retained on purpose

The worktree at `scratchpad/rasen-live` (branch `agent/live-run`) is NOT cleaned up: the
ingest process is running out of it. Removing it would stop the only thing making progress.
Its branch carries no commits of its own — it tracks main — so nothing is unlanded there.
Whoever finishes the run should remove both."}]
